{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed, where right-skewness is defined as assigning a greater portion of probability to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones. In this context, unfavorable outcomes are more negative (larger losses), and favorable outcomes are less negative (smaller or zero losses). For Lottery A: it yields -3000 Euros with 100% probability — a certain loss, so it has no variation and thus no skewness in the probabilistic sense (it is degenerate). For Lottery B: it yields -4000 Euros with 80% probability (unfavorable outcome) and 0 Euros with 20% probability (favorable outcome relative to losses). Since 80% of the probability is on the worse outcome (-4000), this indicates right-skewness. Comparing the two, Lottery B is right-skewed while Lottery A is certain. The decision reason would prefer the more right-skewed lottery, which is B. Thus, the reason can be applied, and it would favor Lottery B.",
