  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed, defined as assigning a greater portion of probability to unfavorable outcomes (i.e., losses) compared to favorable ones. In this context, both lotteries involve only zero or negative outcomes (0 or a loss), so all non-zero outcomes are unfavorable. Lottery A has a 0.2% chance of a -3000 loss and 99.8% chance of 0. Lottery B has a 0.1% chance of a -6000 loss and 99.9% chance of 0. Thus, Lottery A has a higher probability of loss (0.2% vs 0.1%), but a smaller loss magnitude. Since right-skewness in this context would imply a higher concentration of probability on the unfavorable (loss) side, Lottery A is more right-skewed because it has twice the probability of a loss (albeit a smaller one). Therefore, the decision reason would prefer Lottery A.",
  "step_2": "The individual states that the probability of a loss is very low in both lotteries and that the difference of 0.1 percentage points does not matter in their decision. Instead, they decided based on which lottery would result in a smaller loss. This indicates that the individual focused on the magnitude of the potential loss (-3000 vs -6000) and preferred the smaller loss, disregarding the differences in probabilities. The justification centers on minimizing the worst possible outcome (loss amount), not on the distribution of probabilities or the shape of the outcome distribution.",
